{:edb/db-before-t 1986 :edb/db-after-t 1987 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535299 50 #inst "2026-10-08T21:34:51.846Z" 13194139535299 true] [17592186124286 1100 "Given that $y_1(t) = e^{-t}$ is a solution to the equation $y'' - 3y ' - 4y = 0$, use reduction of order to find a second solution $y_2(t)$ to the equation." 13194139535299 false] [17592186124286 1100 "Given that $y_1(t) = e^{-t}$ is a solution to the equation $y'' - 3y ' - 4y = 0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution $y_2(t)$ to the equation." 13194139535299 true] [17592186124286 1145 "According to the reduction of order method, we let\n\n$$\ny_{2} = u(t)e^{-t}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{2} &= ue^{-t} \\\\\ny_{2}^{′} &= u^{′}e^{-t} - ue^{-t} \\\\\ny_{2}^{″} &= u^{″}e^{-t} - 2u^{′}e^{-t} + ue^{-t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{″} - 3y^{′} - 4y &= 0 \\\\\n(u^{″}e^{-t} - 2u^{′}e^{-t} + ue^{-t}) - 3(u^{′}e^{-t} - ue^{-t}) - 4ue^{-t} &= 0 \\\\\nu^{″}e^{-t} - 5u^{′}e^{-t} &= 0 \\\\\ne^{-t}(u^{″} - 5u^{′}) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\ne^t \\neq 0\n$$\n\nwe must have\n\n$$\nu'' - 5u' = 0\n$$\n\nLet $w = u'$. Then our equation becomes a first-order equation,\n\n$$\nw' - 5w=0\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw = c_1 e^{5t}\n$$\n\nNow, since since $w = u'$, we have\n\n$$\nu' = c_1e^{5t} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad u = \\dfrac{c_1}{5} e^{5t} + c_2\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for\n\n$$\ny_2(t) = u e^{-t}\n$$\n\nbecomes\n\n$$\ny_2(t) = e^{-t}\\left(\\dfrac{c_1}{5} e^{5t} + c_2 \\right)\n$$\n\nWe can choose any values for $c_1$ and $c_2$ to yield a second solution. For example, if we set $c_1 = 5$ and $c_2 = 0$, we get\n\n$$\ny_2(t) = e^{-t} \\cdot e^{5t} = e^{4t}\n$$" 13194139535299 false] [17592186124286 1145 "According to the reduction of order method, we let $y_{2} = u(t)e^{-t}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & =u{e}^{-t} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{-t}-u{e}^{-t} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{-t}-2{u}^{′}{e}^{-t}+u{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}-4y & =0 \\\\ ({u}^{″}{e}^{-t}-2{u}^{′}{e}^{-t}+u{e}^{-t})-3({u}^{′}{e}^{-t}-u{e}^{-t})-4u{e}^{-t} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{-t}-5{u}^{′}{e}^{-t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{-t}({u}^{″}-5{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $e^t \\neq 0,$ we must have\n\n$$\nu'' - 5u' = 0.\n$$\n\nLet $w = u'.$ Then our equation becomes a first-order equation,\n\n$$\nw' - 5w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw = c_1 e^{5t}.\n$$\n\nNow, since since $w = u',$ we have\n\n$$\nu' = c_1e^{5t} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad u = \\dfrac{c_1}{5} e^{5t} + c_2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for $y_2(t) = u e^{-t}$ becomes\n\n$$\ny_2(t) = e^{-t}\\left( \\dfrac{c_1}{5} e^{5t} + c_2 \\right).\n$$\n\nWe can choose any values for $c_1$ and $c_2$ to yield a second solution. For example, if we set $c_1 = 5$ and $c_2 = 0,$ we get\n\n$$\ny_2(t) = e^{-t} \\cdot e^{5t} = e^{4t}.\n$$" 13194139535299 true] [17592186124287 1100 "The function $y_1(x) = e^{3x}$ is a solution to the equation $y'' - 6y' + 9y = 0$. If $y_{2} = u(x) y_1(x)$ is a second solution of the equation, then which of the following equations must be satisfied by $u(x)$?\n\n1. $u''=0$\n2. $e^{3x}u'' + u=0$\n3. $u''-u=0$" 13194139535299 false] [17592186124287 1100 "The function $y_1(x) = e^{3x}$ is a solution to the equation $y'' - 6y' + 9y = 0.$ If $y_{2} = u(x) y_1(x)$ is a second solution of the equation, then which of the following equations must be satisfied by $u(x)?$\n\n- $u''=0$\n\n- $e^{3x}u'' + u=0$\n\n- $u''-u=0$" 13194139535299 true] [17592186124287 1145 "We're given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{2}(x) &= u(x)y_{1}(x) \\\\\n&= u(x)e^{3x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{2} &= ue^{3x} \\\\\ny_{2}^{′} &= u^{′}e^{3x} + 3ue^{3x} \\\\\ny_{2}^{″} &= u^{″}e^{3x} + 6u^{′}e^{3x} + 9ue^{3x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{″} - 6y^{′} + 9y &= 0 \\\\\n(u^{″}e^{3x} + 6u^{′}e^{3x} + 9ue^{3x}) - 6(u^{′}e^{3x} + 3ue^{3x}) + 9ue^{3x} &= 0 \\\\\ne^{3x}(u^{″} + 6u^{′} + 9u - 6u^{′} - 18u + 9u) &= 0 \\\\\ne^{3x}u^{″} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\ne^{3x} \\neq 0\n$$\n\nwe must have\n\n$$\nu'' = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139535299 false] [17592186124287 1145 "We're given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =u(x){y}_{1}(x) \\\\  & =u(x){e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & =u{e}^{3x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3u{e}^{3x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{3x}+6{u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+9u{e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}-6{y}^{′}+9y & =0 \\\\ ({u}^{″}{e}^{3x}+6{u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+9u{e}^{3x})-6({u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3u{e}^{3x})+9u{e}^{3x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{3x}({u}^{″}+6{u}^{′}+9u-6{u}^{′}-18u+9u) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{3x}{u}^{″} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $e^{3x} \\neq 0,$ we must have\n\n$$\nu'' = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139535299 true] [17592186124483 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342530 13194139535299 true] [17592186124483 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342537 13194139535299 true] [17592186124483 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342544 13194139535299 true] [17592186124483 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342551 13194139535299 true] [17592186124484 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342509 13194139535299 true] [17592186124484 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342516 13194139535299 true] [17592186124484 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342523 13194139535299 true] [17592186124484 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342558 13194139535299 true] [17592186125098 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535299 true] [17592186125098 1100 "The function $y_{1}(x) = x^{3}$ is a solution to the equation $x^{2}y^{″} - xy^{′} - 3y = 0$ for $x > 0$. Use reduction of order to find a second solution $y_{2}(x)$ to the equation." 13194139535299 false] [17592186125098 1100 "The function ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{3}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}-3y=0$ for $x>0.$ Use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation." 13194139535299 true] [17592186125098 1145 "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x)\\cdot {x}^{3}.$ \n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u(x){x}^{3}$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{x}^{3}+3u{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+6ux.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+6ux)-x({u}^{′}{x}^{3}+3u{x}^{2})-3u{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}+6{u}^{′}x+6u-{u}^{′}x-3u-3u) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{4}({u}^{″}x+5{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{4}\\ne 0$ for $x>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}x+5{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}x+5w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{x}^{-5}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{x}^{-5}\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{1}{4}{c}_{1}{x}^{-4}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{x}^{3}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}(-\\frac{1}{4}{c}_{1}{x}^{-4}+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${c}_{1}=-4$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$" 13194139535299 true] [17592186125100 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535299 true] [17592186125100 1100 "The function $y_{1}(x) = x$ is a solution to the equation $x^{2}y^{″} + 5xy^{′} - 5y = 0$ for $x > 0$. If $y_{2} = u(x)y_{1}(x)$ is a second solution of the equation, then which of the following equations must be satisfied by $u(x)$?" 13194139535299 false] [17592186125100 1100 "The function ${y}_{1}(x)=x$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}+5x{y}^{′}-5y=0$ for $x>0.$ If ${y}_{2}=u(x){y}_{1}(x)$ is a second solution of the equation, then which of the following equations must be satisfied by $u(x)?$" 13194139535299 true] [17592186125100 1145 "We're given that\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=u(x){y}_{1}(x)=ux.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=ux$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}x+u \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}x+2{u}^{′}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}({u}^{″}x+2{u}^{′})+5x({u}^{′}x+u)-5ux & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{x}^{3}+2{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+5{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+5ux-5ux & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{x}^{3}+7{u}^{′}{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}({u}^{″}x+7{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{2}\\ne 0$ for $x>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\nx{u}^{″}+7{u}^{′}=0.\n$$" 13194139535299 true] [17592186342509 1017 #edb/ref 17592186342510 13194139535299 true] [17592186342509 1089 "q-102792" 13194139535299 true] [17592186342509 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535299 true] [17592186342509 1100 "The function ${y}_{1}(t)={e}^{-3t}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}-3y=0.$ If ${y}_{2}=u(t){y}_{1}(t)$ is a second solution of the equation, then which of the following equations must be satisfied by $u(t)?$" 13194139535299 true] [17592186342509 1113 #uuid "b0750bb6-59a2-532b-9169-c3620b5de961" 13194139535299 true] [17592186342509 1145 "We're given that\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=u(t){y}_{1}(t)=u{e}^{-3t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u{e}^{-3t}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{-3t}-3u{e}^{-3t} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{-3t}-6{u}^{′}{e}^{-6t}+9u{e}^{-3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}{e}^{-3t}-6{u}^{′}{e}^{-6t}+9u{e}^{-3t})+2({u}^{′}{e}^{-3t}-3u{e}^{-3t})-3u{e}^{-3t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{-3t}({u}^{″}-6{u}^{′}+9u+2{u}^{′}-6u-3u) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{-3t}({u}^{″}-4{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{-3t}\\ne 0$ for all $t,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}-4{u}^{′}=0.\n$$" 13194139535299 true] [17592186342510 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342511 13194139535299 true] [17592186342510 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342512 13194139535299 true] [17592186342510 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342513 13194139535299 true] [17592186342510 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342514 13194139535299 true] [17592186342510 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342515 13194139535299 true] [17592186342510 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535299 true] [17592186342510 1041 "selection" 13194139535299 true] [17592186342510 1104 #edb/ref 17592186342515 13194139535299 true] [17592186342510 1110 #uuid "0ac83e7f-8531-5999-a8c1-f62b282b1671" 13194139535299 true] [17592186342511 1034 "{u}^{″}-8{u}^{′}=0" 13194139535299 true] [17592186342511 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535299 true] [17592186342511 1111 #uuid "27bff131-1e20-5aaa-87d6-56abf25934fb" 13194139535299 true] [17592186342512 1034 "{u}^{″}+6{u}^{′}-2u=0" 13194139535299 true] [17592186342512 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535299 true] [17592186342512 1111 #uuid "cd17fe2e-54cd-5afd-ae1f-ec24b6b1d796" 13194139535299 true] [17592186342513 1034 "{u}^{″}-6{u}^{′}=0" 13194139535299 true] [17592186342513 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535299 true] [17592186342513 1111 #uuid "92430325-68ed-5de2-8547-5785ecf9194b" 13194139535299 true] [17592186342514 1034 "{u}^{″}+2{u}^{′}+2u=0" 13194139535299 true] [17592186342514 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535299 true] [17592186342514 1111 #uuid "b1de963b-3f55-52da-8196-11499511b9d0" 13194139535299 true] [17592186342515 1034 "{u}^{″}-4{u}^{′}=0" 13194139535299 true] [17592186342515 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535299 true] [17592186342515 1111 #uuid "c8588df4-2eb2-507c-98f3-c1063bd26d10" 13194139535299 true] [17592186342516 1017 #edb/ref 17592186342517 13194139535299 true] [17592186342516 1089 "q-102793" 13194139535299 true] [17592186342516 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535299 true] [17592186342516 1100 "The function ${y}_{1}(t)={t}^{-2}$ is a solution to the equation ${t}^{2}{y}^{″}+2t{y}^{′}-2y=0$ for $t>0.$ If ${y}_{2}=u(t){y}_{1}(t)$ is a second solution of the equation, then which of the following equations must be satisfied by $u(t)?$" 13194139535299 true] [17592186342516 1113 #uuid "fd891361-e379-54a9-be99-b7079ca7d049" 13194139535299 true] [17592186342516 1145 "We're given that\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=u(t){y}_{1}(t)=u{t}^{-2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u{t}^{-2}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{t}^{-2}-2u{t}^{-3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{t}^{-2}-4{u}^{′}{t}^{-3}+6u{t}^{-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}({u}^{″}{t}^{-2}-4{u}^{′}{t}^{-3}+6u{t}^{-4})+2t({u}^{′}{t}^{-2}-2u{t}^{-3})-2u{t}^{-2} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}-4{u}^{′}{t}^{-1}+6u{t}^{-2}+2{u}^{′}{t}^{-1}-4u{t}^{-2}-2u{t}^{-2} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}-2{u}^{′}{t}^{-1} & =0 \\\\ {t}^{-1}({u}^{″}t-2{u}^{′}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{t}({u}^{″}t-2{u}^{′}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{1}{t}\\ne 0$ for $t>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\nt{u}^{″}-2{u}^{′}=0.\n$$" 13194139535299 true] [17592186342517 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342518 13194139535299 true] [17592186342517 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342519 13194139535299 true] [17592186342517 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342520 13194139535299 true] [17592186342517 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342521 13194139535299 true] [17592186342517 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342522 13194139535299 true] [17592186342517 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535299 true] [17592186342517 1041 "selection" 13194139535299 true] [17592186342517 1104 #edb/ref 17592186342521 13194139535299 true] [17592186342517 1110 #uuid "fd6df1d1-8b18-593a-94b2-27972f62bdb9" 13194139535299 true] [17592186342518 1034 "{u}^{″}-{t}^{2}{u}^{′}=0" 13194139535299 true] [17592186342518 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535299 true] [17592186342518 1111 #uuid "49241e1a-112f-520a-a